WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.200
 Okay, I think I will talk to you today about how we might design in consideration of the

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:33.920
 future, and why thinking about the future is useful in your design experiments that you're

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.640
 working on now.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:45.560
 So to get started, just sort of think about what do you picture when you hear the future?

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.640
 Is it perhaps robots running around helping us all the time?

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:58.360
 Is it maybe this space where we are starting to bring the technological and the natural

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.360
 closer together?

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:10.080
 Do you think exclusively about goofy stainless steel cars from the 80s?

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:20.800
 Maybe it's we're all doing Google Glass VR headsets everywhere.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.800
 Maybe it's like we're all in the Matrix.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.220
 You might also have more of a utopian or like positive version where the future has solved

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:40.440
 a lot of people's problems, or maybe it's dystopian and the future is just rampant with humanity's

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.080
 problems destroying the world.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.220
 This ironically is not a future photo.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:02:00.160
 So it's a past photo from the wildfires in 2020 in California.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:08.680
 Maybe our view of the future is we've left, you know, we're all one Earth in harmony together,

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:17.400
 or maybe it's that we've kind of abandoned Earth for the stars.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:24.020
 And so what's hard about talking about the future is that what it really is, at least

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:31.540
 in English, as a word it just means what is next, like what's going to happen, what is

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:32.540
 going to be.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:39.220
 It's not really a defined space, it's just what we haven't gotten to yet.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:46.940
 And that means that a lot of choices we make now might lead to very different kinds of futures.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:56.020
 I think the other thing to think about when you think about the future is the past also

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:03.740
 comes along with the future.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:09.020
 So how do we make sense of future possibilities?

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:14.400
 In this evolved out of something called forecasting.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:23.540
 And the main way that people, the main way, useful way I've seen people kind of like help each

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:30.980
 other think about different futures is using this tool called the future cone.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:39.380
 So time is a thing that, at least according to how we experience it, moves forward.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:48.100
 So in my timeline, it's moving to the right, and we're experiencing now.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.100
 Right?

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:55.540
 It's now, now, and now it's now again, and now it's now again.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:04.260
 So anything before, anything that's happened before now is the past.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.760
 Anything that happens after now is the future.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:15.260
 Because we're always like slowly time, you know, ever so slowly time traveling into the future.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.260
 Right?

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:22.260
 Because it's now again.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:31.260
 And so the future, you can't guarantee what exactly will happen, which means everything

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.260
 in the future is a potential outcome.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:51.980
 And the farther and farther ahead into the future, you get the wider and wider those outcomes become.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:57.980
 And so there's going to be a bunch of P words describing the kind of categories of outcomes.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.860
 So at the kind of most lame is the projected future.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:04.700
 This is just the default future.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:12.700
 If nothing changes, and we just kind of extrapolate the present day out, this is the future that we will reach.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:33.420
 I find that this is not a particularly interesting place to think about, other than that, like, I don't know about you, but I don't particularly want the present just extrapolated out forever into the future, because there's a bunch of things I don't like about the present.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:50.140
 But, like, I know that I'm going to reach this conclusion slide at some point in this talk, and so I know that that's a projected future that we'll all arrive at together.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:06:01.100
 Certainty lives in this place to a certain extent, especially in the near future.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.660
 Now, what most people are talking about when they talk about the future is the probable future.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.460
 This is, like, predictions about what is likely to happen.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:14.460
 You know, it's highly likely that, like...

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.140
 Well, I need some example.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:24.300
 Or, it's highly likely that X, and I can put an image on there.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:40.800
 There's the plausible what we believe could happen with what we know and with the technology we have today, and so this is like, you know, there's a new MNRA vaccine for potentially preventing cancers.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:58.200
 Maybe it is plausible that there is some kind of intervention that we come up with for that that's starting to live pretty far out from the projected or even the probable future, however.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:04.480
 But it's into the plausible, the realm of, like, this could happen if we can keep investing money or if we keep doing this.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:20.440
 And then there's the possible in general, where it's not impossible, but it's highly unlikely given current research or abilities.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:27.760
 And so this is, like, futures that we still might think could happen, but it needs some future knowledge we don't possess.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.200
 This is, like, the United States becomes all renewable energy-powered.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:38.780
 Well, frankly, that's even just plausible.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:40.340
 Like, we can do it.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:54.120
 But is it possible, like, a lot of behaviors have to change to make that happen, which maybe is even harder than just, like, inventing new technology?

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:03.420
 And then there's the preposterous, which these are things that, at least in the present, we say these are ridiculous or these are impossible or these will never happen.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:06.020
 This could be the realm of fantasy.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:15.980
 This could also just be that, like, people aren't thinking very interestingly about what's around them.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:24.980
 Many of the things that we have in our immediate present are things that, even a few decades ago, were preposterous.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:49.460
 And then one other sort of subspace, which isn't, like, a whole section of the cone, but is maybe, like, a bunch of different little places in and amongst the cone, is the preferable.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:55.260
 And so this is, like, what you specifically want or think should or ought to happen.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.000
 This could be preposterous as well.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:17.140
 And that might mean that you're just projecting your own ideals or your own concepts, which is fine, frankly, from the perspective of being an artist or a designer who can actually create things to explain their ideas.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:26.280
 Um, so I didn't originate this, the person I got these ideas from was Joseph Voros.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:34.260
 And I got led to his version of these things from Don and Robbie, who got their ideas from this guy, Stuart Candy.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:47.640
 So, whew, that's a lot of future cone relationships.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:53.360
 So, why should we think about future possibles at all?

00:09:53.360 --> 00:10:11.100
 So, for one, I think, if you want to help maintain Spaceship Earth, Buckminster Fuller talked about how Earth needs a better operating manual.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:22.980
 And we're all Earthstronauts flying around space on Spaceship Earth, the same way that astronauts fly in the space shuttle because there's no resupply mission coming.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.900
 We have to figure out how to make this planet work cyclically.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:54.040
 So, I like to think about preferable futures, like sustainable ones, or circular ones, or less, you know, like a decolonized versions of the future, decarbonized versions of the future.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:11:03.040
 And I kind of arrived at these, thinking through this book, spiked into everything that I read when I was in grad school.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:13.940
 And so, Dune and Robbie are a design team, design duo, who use their skills as designers to kind of come up with

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:30.460
 projects that critique the current view of mainstream designing.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:43.200
 This was called robots, and so these are all different robots that humans actually have to, like, directly interact with to trigger the task or the thing that the robot does.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:54.620
 And so they're not, like, autonomous robots, they're robots that, like, humans can wield control over.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:55.820
 Control is maybe not the right word.

00:11:55.820 --> 00:12:06.040
 But they're not, you know, walking, talking, murdering versions of artificial intelligence that are able to just roam.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:22.160
 They're like robots that co-exist as house products instead, which is maybe more interesting than the kind of HomePod things we've ended up with from Google and Amazon and Apple.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:29.420
 Anyway, they have more fantastical projects.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:52.380
 Like, this is humans in a future world where we need a lot more help finding edible food, and so these are all people with suits that enable food foraging from the sort of wastelands that are left more easily.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:13:16.380
 And then I think the project that I kind of got turned on by them was this one called the United Micro Kingdoms, and again, it's sort of using design to both create a kind of semiotic language to describe this future, but also, like, really pretty clearly articulate what's special or unique about these four different

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:44.380
 different versions of a new UK in the not so distant future, but they call this speculative design, and they evolved that from actually originally kind of calling it critical design, and so to them, this is design where they're making things in the present, but the present

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:51.380
 things are intended to be critiques of projected futures.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:14:09.380
 So, this could also be reframed as like, can we design things in the present that point us to envisioned futures, and ultimately, these are really just designs that challenge the kind of current status quo ideas or current projected future ideas.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:36.380
 And sort of reframed as maybe also just like design that actually tries to provide you options beyond the sort of typical moderno, techno, capitalist set of options that like, the existing piles of things on target shelves and Amazon carts do.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:55.380
 And so again, and so we can apply this back to the future cone, each design project might live in like a different place more or less plausible, but more or less positive or negative on the future cone.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:15:02.380
 So, a different way this is described is by this guy, Julian Bleeker, he calls it design fiction.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:09.380
 He gets, he got into this because he like loved Star Trek as a kid and got like a book of all of the,

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:36.380
 all of the, like the fan fiction, the fan made book of like Star Trek branding guidelines that also has like, you know, schematics for things like the communicators and stuff in that series.

00:15:36.380 --> 00:16:01.380
 And this idea that like you could get so into a science fiction show that he would make a brand guideline about how to use the typography for that thing was really interesting to him and he, and he sort of grew that into working with giant clients in a variety of different ways with sort of a similar speculative touch.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:14.380
 And so he refers to design fictions as, um, they're objects from near futures that sort of ask us what directions things can go or evolve.

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.380
 So this is a project he was working on with Ikea.

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:28.380
 Um, and these are like example objects from that Ikea could sell.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:42.380
 Um, and that turned into this like Ikea near future catalog, which at the surface just sort of looks like a regular catalog.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:57.380
 But then you see that like there's some sort of flair coming out of this magazine they're looking at all of these things about kitchen stuff is based on like renting it by the hour.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:01.380
 It's like so many credits per hour to use.

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:09.380
 And this guy is sitting in like with VR glasses and like a giant projection on his wall instead of just being in a normal office.

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:28.380
 And so Julian bleaker sort of version of this is like real normal design objects that are not confusing at all, but the sort of like sneak in these other ways you might be thinking about or potentially helping you picture how the future would be different.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:39.380
 He's also thinking about how it's the same like just because we're 10 years in the future or 50 years in the future doesn't mean that everything else from the present is also gone or missing.

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:51.380
 And so how do these future ideas sort of also fit into our regular everyday things.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:58.380
 So the sort of other way that bleaker describes himself is being an archeologist or an anthropologist of the future.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:18.380
 Like we've taken our weird little time machine to the future and we're in this room and we see a couple of little tidbits that like clue us in on what the new politics or processes or whatever of this future world are.

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:24.380
 And so that means another way to think about you as designers thinking about whatever world building you're doing in your projects.

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:38.380
 And how do you like hint or clue in people to those things without having to do tons of exposition or tons of explanation every time you need somebody to kind of get there with you.

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:47.380
 So tangible prototypes of material cultural artifacts of possible near futures.

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:51.380
 That's what bleaker is saying a design fiction is.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:57.380
 I think this fits really nicely with making design projects especially in a thesis context.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:00.380
 Okay.

